  "body": "**Replaces #436**, which was opened from the old fork. GitHub detached that fork from this repo's network when NeoTech/Camcraft was briefly private, so it can no longer be updated. **Now stacked on #473** (the replacement for #399). It carries the merges with main through #461: `ToolBody` keeps #395's lathe frame and #431's insert zone alongside this PR's `spun` field. A file with `spun` together with `lathe` or `insert_zone` is refused by name.\n\n**Stacked on #399** (tool editor body column), which is on #381 and #348. First slice of #93: the **holder** of a milling tool drawn as a revolved half-outline. The custom cutter/shank profile is a follow-up, hence `Refs #93`.\n\n## What changed\n- **`crates/tools/src/spun.rs`: `SpunProfile`**, a (z, r, arc) half-outline.\n  - **Refusals by name, with 1-based point numbers:** 3..64 points; finite; r ≥ 0, z ≥ 0; closed to the axis at both ends; no interior point on the axis; segments ≥ 0.01 mm; an arc no tighter than its chord; the outline never running back down the axis; not flat; size caps.\n  - **Arcs never under-read:** convex arcs are replaced by the polygon that encloses them, concave arcs by chords on the arc.\n  - Its `bands()` feed #381's `HolderProfile` exactly.\n- **Storage: a drawn holder is a `ToolBody`.** The outline's own bands are its envelope, so every #368 law reads it unchanged: fixture bands, `enclose`, the stamp, the fits/flutes/lathe refusals, undo and delete. Its facets are the revolve of the outline, so the sim draws it.\n  - A new optional `ToolBody::spun` keeps the points for re-editing.\n  - **No project version rung:** `ToolBodyDto` gets an optional `spun` key beside the facets, which are still written.\n    - A reader that knows the key redraws the body from it.\n    - A reader that doesn't judges the facets. Their ring points sit on the outline, so it can only over-report. A test pins this.\n  - **Parametric means no body,** so a tool that never went Custom keeps identical stamps and toolpaths.\n- **UI, milling kinds only.** The third column gets **Body / Profile** tabs; turning tools show the Body section unchanged, per the owner's comment on #93. The Profile tab has:\n  - a Parametric / Custom toggle;\n  - a points table with insert and remove (read-only in Parametric);\n  - a to-scale side view: cutter and shank from the numbers, the holder amber when valid and red when not, flute/stickout/gauge ticks;\n  - a live refusal by name;\n  - **Apply profile**, which issues one undoable `SetToolBody::spun`.\n\n  Parametric follows the draft numbers live; Custom keeps hand edits.\n- `docs/decisions/93-spun-tool-editor.md`\n\n## How tested\n- tools lib: 21 pass (6 new).\n- `tool_spun_profile` (6 tests):\n  - bands exact;\n  - a drawn flange is flagged by the fixture laws;\n  - a parametric holder equals `HolderStack::cylinder`, and the stamp is unchanged after clearing;\n  - undo and redo;\n  - refusals, including into the flutes and on a lathe kind;\n  - round-trip byte-identical, and a reader without the `spun` key over-reports.\n- `tool_body` (8), `tool_body_view` (8) and `project_roundtrip` (69) pass.\n- ui `tool_` tests: 22 pass. `tool_editor_snapshot` fails on purpose (release-lane, #359), because the tab row is new.\n- `clippy -p camcraft-tools -p camcraft-app -p camcraft-ui --tests -D warnings` is clean.\n- `scripts/gate.sh batch` fails only on tests already red on main:\n  - #415;\n  - the headless-GPU ui tests;\n  - the release-lane snapshot;\n  - `swarf_pose_continuity` (PR #351).\n\n## Not exercised (draft until the contributor's GUI check; a test kit is prepared)\nFollow-ups:\n1. A custom cutter/neck/shank profile (`SpunProfile::cutter_of` is in place).\n2. Fillet-at-vertex arcs.\n3. Dragging points in the side view.\n4. Spun profiles in the tool library file (#348).\n\nChangelog: The tool editor draws a milling tool's holder as a revolved outline (points, arcs, live side view); the collision checks judge the drawn shape (#93)\n\nRefs #93\n\n🤖 Generated with [Claude Code](https://claude.com/claude-code)\n\n",
